{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The built-in style variants, alternative Loading placeholders, and the theming configuration point — everything beyond the out-of-the-box defaults for tuning how a placeholder looks without writing a fully custom one."}],"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"level":2,"anchor":"Overview","type":"heading","text":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Every default placeholder ships two independent levers: a "},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"style"}],"type":"strong"},{"type":"text","text":" (how big a footprint it expects to own) and, in SwiftUI, a "},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"theme"}],"type":"strong"},{"type":"text","text":" (what color\/font it uses). On top of the default spinner, two alternative Loading placeholders cover the other common shapes a loading state takes: a pulsing skeleton, and a redacted mirror of the real content."}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"`.compact` style","anchor":"compact-style"},{"inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatePlaceholderStyle"},{"type":"text","text":" controls how much space a default placeholder expects to fill. Every default placeholder’s initializer takes a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"style:"},{"type":"text","text":" parameter, defaulting to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".standard"},{"type":"text","text":" — the full-screen treatment: a full-size icon, and, for the Loading spinner, filling all available space."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Pass "},{"code":".compact","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" to embed the same placeholder in a card or section instead of a whole screen: a smaller icon, smaller text, and — for the Loading spinner — no more forcing itself to fill the available space:"}]},{"code":["ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView(style: .compact)","ScreenStateDefaultLoadingUIView(style: .compact)"],"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":".compact"},{"type":"text","text":" always keeps a vertical icon-above-text layout, regardless of the device’s orientation — a card’s size is dictated by the card, not the device, unlike the automatic landscape-iPhone icon-beside-text switch "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".standard"},{"type":"text","text":" makes on its own."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Alternative Loading placeholders","anchor":"Alternative-Loading-placeholders"},{"inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingView"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingUIView"},{"type":"text","text":" — the spinner — are what every default-wired "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateView"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateContainerView"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateViewController"},{"type":"text","text":" shows. Two opt-in alternatives cover the other common shapes a loading state takes:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateShimmerLoadingView","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateShimmerLoadingUIView","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":": a pulsing skeleton of a circular avatar plus two text-line placeholders, for a generic “something’s coming” look that doesn’t need to know the real content’s shape.","type":"text"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateRedactedLoadingView","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":": renders your real content view through SwiftUI’s ","type":"text"},{"code":".redacted(reason: .placeholder)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", so the placeholder mirrors the exact shape of the content it’s standing in for. SwiftUI-only — ","type":"text"},{"code":"redacted(reason:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" has no UIKit equivalent.","type":"text"}]}]}],"type":"unorderedList"},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["ScreenStateShimmerLoadingView()","","ScreenStateRedactedLoadingView {","    ArticleRow(article: .placeholder)","}"]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Both respect Reduce Motion and Increase Contrast the same way the default spinner does; see "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Accessibility","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"Theming","type":"heading","text":"Theming","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatesTheme","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" lets an app theme every default placeholder at once — "},{"code":"tint","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"code":"titleFont","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", and "},{"code":"messageFont","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" — instead of passing a fully custom placeholder view at every call site. Any property left "},{"code":"nil","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" (the default, via "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatesTheme\/standard","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":") keeps the built-in gradient\/font. Increase Contrast still wins over "},{"code":"tint","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" regardless, in both paradigms below — it’s an accessibility need, not a branding preference."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"SwiftUI","type":"heading","text":"SwiftUI","level":3},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Set a theme via "},{"code":"View.screenStatesTheme(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" on a container view, and every default placeholder beneath it in the view hierarchy (including "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateShimmerLoadingView","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":") picks it up through SwiftUI’s "},{"code":"Environment","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":":"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["MyScreenView()","    .screenStatesTheme(ScreenStatesTheme(tint: .indigo, titleFont: .title2, messageFont: .callout))"]},{"anchor":"UIKit","type":"heading","text":"UIKit","level":3},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatesThemeTrait"},{"type":"text","text":" is the UIKit counterpart, using UIKit’s trait system in place of an "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Environment"},{"type":"text","text":". Set it once via "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"traitOverrides"},{"type":"text","text":" on a container view or view controller, and every default UIKit placeholder beneath it picks it up the same way, through "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"traitCollection"},{"type":"text","text":":"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["someContainerView.traitOverrides.screenStatesTheme = ScreenStatesTheme(tint: .indigo)"]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Only "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"tint"},{"type":"text","text":" carries over on the UIKit side — there’s no public "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Font"},{"type":"text","text":" → "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIFont"},{"type":"text","text":" bridging API, so "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"titleFont"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"messageFont"},{"type":"text","text":" stay SwiftUI-only."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":3,"anchor":"Mixing-both-paradigms","type":"heading","text":"Mixing both paradigms"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A custom trait like "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatesThemeTrait"},{"type":"text","text":" isn’t automatically bridged to or from SwiftUI’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Environment"},{"type":"text","text":" the way a few of Apple’s own built-in traits are. On a screen that embeds one paradigm inside the other — a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIHostingController"},{"type":"text","text":" in a UIKit navigation stack, or a UIKit view wrapped in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIViewRepresentable"},{"type":"text","text":" inside a SwiftUI screen — set the theme explicitly at the embedding point rather than relying on either ambient value to cross over on its own. See "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatesThemeTrait"},{"type":"text","text":"’s documentation for the exact pattern in both directions."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"}],"sections":[],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Styling"},"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}],"title":"Styling","roleHeading":"Article","role":"article"},"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/styling"]}],"kind":"article","seeAlsoSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingView","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultError